      "question": "What other equations might we need to rely on when applying Newton’s second law to solve a problem?",
      "reference": "To apply Newton’s second law as the primary equation in solving a problem, we sometimes have to rely on other equations, such as the one for weight or one of the kinematic equations, to complete the solution.",
      "usable": true
    },
    {
      "passage": 5,
      "question": "Why is it helpful to circle the object in gray when first learning to draw free-body diagrams?",
      "reference": "This focuses your attention, preventing you from considering forces that are not acting on the body.",
      "usable": true
    },
    {
      "passage": 6,
      "question": "What is the easiest method for solving two-dimensional vector problems where no two vectors are parallel?",
      "reference": "Whenever we have two-dimensional vector problems in which no two vectors are parallel, the easiest method of solution is to pick a convenient coordinate system and project the vectors onto its axes.",
      "usable": true
    },
    {
      "passage": 7,
      "question": "How long will it take the soccer player to reach the ball if she experiences a force of 126 N and is 2.00 m away?",
      "reference": "If she now experiences a force of 126 N to attempt to steal the ball, which is 2.00 m away from her, how long will it take her to get to the ball?",
      "usable": true
    },
    {
      "passage": 8,
      "question": "How does the mass of an object differ from its weight depending on location?",
      "reference": "Weight is the pull of Earth on an object. It depends on the distance from the center of Earth. Unlike weight, mass does not vary with location.",
      "usable": true
    },
    {
      "passage": 9,
      "question": "What can we infer about the net horizontal force on a car if it is moving at a constant velocity?",
      "reference": "What does the knowledge that the car is moving at constant velocity tell us about the net horizontal force on the car compared to the friction force?",
      "usable": true
    },
    {
      "passage": 10,
      "question": "What is the resulting acceleration when a force of 51 N is applied to a 24 kg mass?",
      "reference": "$$ a = \\frac{51 \\text{ kg} \\cdot \\text{m/s}^2}{24 \\text{ kg}} = 2.1 \\text{ m/s}^2 $$",
      "usable": true
    }
  ]
